WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.800
Many enterprises today are getting advantages&nbsp;
out of moving workloads into the public cloud.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.960
They're building cognitive applications, they're&nbsp;
scaling them on demand, and they're improving the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:14.160
speed of their development through as a service&nbsp;
API-based consumption on the public cloud. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.360
the reality is many workloads have not moved to&nbsp;
the public cloud yet many of those workloads are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.920
from regulated industries, they have security&nbsp;
and compliance requirements, or performance and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.960
latency requirements, that make it challenging to&nbsp;
physically move those applications into a public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.760
cloud data center. And what many organizations&nbsp;
really want is all of the agility benefits of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:39.520
public cloud, but the flexibility to run those&nbsp;
workloads wherever they need them: on-premises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.640
in their existing data centers, in multiple public&nbsp;
cloud providers around the world, or at the edge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.360
of the network closer to the applications&nbsp;
and data sources that they need to process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.000
Public cloud is evolving to satisfy this&nbsp;
requirement with a new concept called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.680
“Distributed Cloud”.&nbsp;So, the heart of IBM Cloud&nbsp;
Satellite is the notion of IBM Cloud Services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:05.920
managed anywhere. When you consume capabilities on&nbsp;
the IBM Cloud today you have a catalog of over 130&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:11.680
services that you can use to build and run your&nbsp;
applications - everything from infrastructure to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:18.240
Kubernetes and OpenShift databases, DevOps tools,&nbsp;
and frameworks for AI machine learning and IOT.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.080
All of those services are available as APIs that&nbsp;
you can provision on demand, and you can consume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.480
and use to build your applications. With IBM&nbsp;
Cloud Satellite, we're extending that catalog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:33.760
of services and enabling it to be consumed&nbsp;
in the exact same way, through the same APIs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:39.680
in locations outside of IBM Cloud regions. So,&nbsp;
you can now consume an OpenShift cluster in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:46.640
data center, or AI machine learning framework in&nbsp;
your factory. You can use those capabilities in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:53.200
consistent way wherever the application workload&nbsp;
needs to run. You can also use software - things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:58.800
like IBM Cloud Paks and open-source capabilities&nbsp;
- and deploy them in a consistent way across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.240
any environment. One of the advantages of this&nbsp;
approach is that the public cloud becomes kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:09.360
the central management, or control plane, for all&nbsp;
of your distributed workloads. You have a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:15.120
console that you can log into with IBM Cloud&nbsp;
that allows you to provision resources, provision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.680
cloud services, configure them, provision your&nbsp;
applications, and manage them in a consistent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.640
way across this diverse set of environments.&nbsp; You&nbsp;
get a single way to do security. You also get,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.040
of course, common observability - logging&nbsp;
and monitoring, and dashboards and alerts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.040
that allow you to monitor the workloads that&nbsp;
you're running in a consistent way across all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:38.080
these environments.&nbsp; We're also doing some work&nbsp;
in Satellite to help you with inventory and change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.440
management. So, part of the power of Satellite is&nbsp;
this common control plane or single pane of glass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:48.160
that allows you to manage your applications across&nbsp;
a diverse set of environments. Now, how does this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:53.520
work?&nbsp; Kind of the key idea within Satellite that&nbsp;
we're introducing is the notion of a location.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:59.920
A location is a way to define, to IBM Cloud, a&nbsp;
place outside of IBM Cloud where you want to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:05.120
able to deploy and consume cloud services. It&nbsp;
is a collection of infrastructure that you own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.880
that we're going to use on your behalf to run&nbsp;
cloud services. That location is essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:16.640
a collection of Linux hosts, of virtual machines&nbsp;
or physical machines, that get arranged together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:22.400
into a pool of resources, that get managed&nbsp;
automatically by Satellite, and that get used,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:27.600
by us, to provision services.&nbsp;So, when you, let's&nbsp;
say, you want to create a Satellite location in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:32.800
your data center, you can provision a set of Linux&nbsp;
machines within that data center. You register&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:39.360
those Linux machines with IBM Cloud as a location,&nbsp;
and once that collection is registered with IBM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:44.720
Cloud Satellite, you now see that location kind of&nbsp;
as like a custom region within your cloud account,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.640
where you can now deploy cloud services. So,&nbsp;
if you want to create a Postgres database,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.520
define a DevOps tool chain, or create an&nbsp;
OpenShift cluster - when you provision that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.400
cluster through services in the cloud, that custom&nbsp;
location you just defined in your data center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.400
will be a location that you can select&nbsp;
when you provision that resource.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:08.800
And so, locations is a really simple concept where&nbsp;
you're able to take any Linux infrastructure and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.280
make it available as a place to run cloud&nbsp;
services.&nbsp; Now, one of the advantages of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:18.880
this idea of a location is it really provides a&nbsp;
tremendous flexibility. IBM Cloud Satellite is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:24.720
built on top of Kubernetes and OpenShift as the&nbsp;
common infrastructure abstraction that we use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.880
to allow you to consume cloud services in any&nbsp;
infrastructure. And by using OpenShift as that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:36.160
abstraction layer, we can support a variety of&nbsp;
infrastructures underneath a Satellite location.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:40.080
You can bring your own custom infrastructure&nbsp;
in your data center, physical or virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:45.840
servers. You can use your account on&nbsp;
another public cloud like amazon, or azure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.880
or google - and consume that infrastructure,&nbsp;
and arrange it into a location that's used by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:56.800
IBM Cloud for running cloud services.&nbsp; Now, since&nbsp;
those locations, those services and applications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:01.440
are managed by IBM Cloud, we, of course,&nbsp;
need a connection back to the cloud to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:05.520
us manage those things - and that connection&nbsp;
is provided by a component called “Satellite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.320
link”. And the idea of Satellite link is to&nbsp;
give you the transparent visibility to all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:15.440
data that's flowing back and forth between&nbsp;
the cloud and that location, and to give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:21.600
control over what applications and resources are&nbsp;
exposed in those locations.&nbsp;So, these two ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:26.960
of location and link, provide the fundamental new&nbsp;
concepts that Satellite introduces to IBM Cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:32.080
to give you the power of services anywhere. We're&nbsp;
also going to provide some optimized solutions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:38.480
Fully integrated rack systems both from IBM and&nbsp;
from partners, and as-a-service infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:43.360
capabilities where we can run the entire stack&nbsp;
for you from hardware through infrastructure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:49.440
and up into platform and SaaS applications. And&nbsp;
so, you can consume infrastructure either with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:54.000
what you have, you can build new environments,&nbsp;
or you can run multi-cloud environments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:59.120
across IBM Cloud and other public cloud providers.&nbsp;
So, those are kind of the core ideas in Satellite:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:05.360
locations and link allow us to extend our&nbsp;
cloud catalog services to any location,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:10.640
giving you the power of cloud, and the power of&nbsp;
IBM Cloud, anywhere in the world that you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:19.840
it.&nbsp;All you need is some Linux infrastructure&nbsp;
and IBM does the rest. Thanks a lot.

